        'Probability measures the likelihood of an event occurring, expressed as a number between 0 (impossible) and 1 (certain). In finance, nothing is certain, every investment decision involves probabilities. A stock might have a 70% probability of rising 15% and a 30% probability of falling 10%. Understanding these probabilities is the foundation of every quantitative decision.',
        'Expected Value (EV) is the weighted average of all possible outcomes. It tells you what you\'d earn on average if you repeated a decision many times. The formula: $$EV = \\sum_{i} P_i \\times V_i$$ For the stock above: $EV = (0.70 \\times 15\\%) + (0.30 \\times -10\\%) = 10.5\\% - 3\\% = +7.5\\%$. A positive expected value means the bet is worth taking over many repetitions.',
        'The key insight: individual outcomes are unpredictable, but expected value over many decisions is highly predictable. A casino doesn\'t know who will win or lose at any given table, but with a 5.3% edge on roulette across millions of spins, their profit is virtually guaranteed. Ed Thorp realized he could apply the same principle to financial markets, find positive EV situations and repeat them thousands of times.'
