        { term: 'Black-Scholes Model', definition: 'The Nobel Prize-winning mathematical formula for pricing European options based on five inputs: stock price, strike price, time to expiry, risk-free rate, and volatility.', example: 'With Apple at $180, strike $200, 3 months to expiry, 4% risk-free rate, and 25% volatility, Black-Scholes gives a call price of approximately $3.50.' },
        { term: 'Implied Volatility', definition: 'The volatility level that, when plugged into the Black-Scholes formula, produces the current market price of an option. It represents the market\'s expectation of future volatility.', example: 'If a call option trades at $8 but Black-Scholes with 20% volatility prices it at $6, the implied volatility must be higher than 20%, perhaps 28%. The market is pricing in more expected movement.' },
        { term: 'The Greeks', definition: 'Partial derivatives of the option price with respect to each input: Delta (stock price), Gamma (rate of delta change), Theta (time decay), Vega (volatility), Rho (interest rates).', example: 'A Delta of 0.60 means the option price moves $0.60 for every $1 move in the stock. Theta of -0.05 means the option loses $0.05 per day from time decay.' }
