      { term: 'Call Option', definition: 'A contract giving the holder the right (not obligation) to buy an underlying asset at a specified strike price before or at expiration. Profits when the asset price rises above the strike.', example: 'You buy a $100 call on Tesla for $5. If Tesla rises to $120, you exercise and profit $15 ($120-$100-$5). If Tesla stays below $100, you lose the $5 premium.', sentence: 'Buying call options provides leveraged upside exposure: a 10% stock move might produce a 50-100% option return, though the entire premium is at risk.' },
      { term: 'Put Option', definition: 'A contract giving the holder the right (not obligation) to sell an underlying asset at a specified strike price before or at expiration. Profits when the asset price falls below the strike.', example: 'You buy a $100 put on a stock for $3. If the stock drops to $85, you profit $12 ($100-$85-$3). Puts act as "portfolio insurance" against declines.', sentence: 'Institutional investors routinely buy put options as insurance, willing to pay the premium for protection against market crashes.' },
      { term: 'Implied Volatility', definition: 'The market\'s forecast of future volatility, extracted by solving the Black-Scholes formula backward, given the observed option price, what volatility makes the formula produce that price?', example: 'Before Apple\'s earnings, implied volatility on at-the-money options might jump to 50%. After the announcement, it collapses to 25% ("vol crush") as uncertainty resolves.', sentence: 'The VIX index, Wall Street\'s "fear gauge", is calculated entirely from S&P 500 option implied volatilities and represents the market\'s 30-day volatility forecast.' },
      { term: 'Delta (Δ)', definition: 'The sensitivity of an option\'s price to a $1 change in the underlying. Ranges from 0 to 1 for calls, -1 to 0 for puts. Also approximates the probability of finishing in-the-money.', example: 'A call with Delta 0.50 (at-the-money) moves $0.50 for every $1 stock move. Delta hedging means holding -Delta shares per option to create a risk-neutral position.', sentence: 'Market makers delta-hedge their option positions thousands of times per day, buying and selling shares to maintain a net delta near zero and profit purely from the bid-ask spread.' },
      { term: 'Volatility Smile', definition: 'The empirical pattern where implied volatility varies by strike price, typically higher for deep out-of-the-money puts and calls than for at-the-money options. This contradicts Black-Scholes, which assumes constant volatility.', example: 'After the 1987 crash, far out-of-the-money puts traded at 30-40% implied volatility while at-the-money options traded at 15-20%. The "smile" reflects the market pricing in crash risk.', sentence: 'The volatility smile is proof that markets don\'t believe in Black-Scholes\' assumption of normally-distributed returns, they price in fat tails and crash risk.' },
      { term: 'Theta (Θ)', definition: 'The rate at which an option loses value as time passes, with all else unchanged. Always negative for option buyers. Accelerates dramatically in the final weeks before expiration.', example: 'A $5 option with 30 days to expiry might lose $0.05/day initially, but $0.25/day in the final week. This accelerating decay is why short-term options are extremely time-sensitive.', sentence: 'Professional option sellers systematically harvest theta decay by selling options with 30-45 days to expiry and closing positions before the final week\'s accelerated decay.' }
