            { label: 'Adjust model parameters to produce a lower VaR, keeping portfolio managers happy is important for team harmony', consequence: 'This is exactly what happened in the London Whale incident. JPMorgan\'s CIO unit changed their VaR model to reduce reported risk, then lost $6.2 billion. Manipulating risk models to produce desired outputs is the most dangerous thing a risk officer can do, it creates a false sense of security.', score: 0 },
            { label: 'Refuse to change the model, explain that VaR reflects the mathematical reality of the portfolio\'s risk, and present ways to actually reduce risk (position size reductions, hedges) rather than pretend it doesn\'t exist', consequence: 'The correct and courageous response. The CRO\'s job is to measure risk accurately, not to make traders comfortable. If VaR is too high, the solution is to reduce actual risk (smaller positions, better hedges), not to manipulate the model. This is the lesson of every risk management failure in history.', score: 3 },
            { label: 'Compromise, report a VaR halfway between your model\'s output and what the PMs want', consequence: 'There is no legitimate compromise on risk measurement. The VaR is whatever the calibrated model says it is. "Splitting the difference" between the mathematical answer and the desired answer is just a smaller version of model manipulation. Risk numbers must be honest or they\'re useless.', score: 0 }
