      'Using fat-tailed distributions, refusing to manipulate VaR, and implementing stress testing with regime detection, scoring 9/9.',
      'The history of quantitative risk management is a history of model failures followed by improvements. Every major financial crisis, 1987, 1998 (LTCM), 2008, the London Whale, was partly caused by over-reliance on Monte Carlo models with unrealistic assumptions. The lesson: Monte Carlo simulation is an extraordinarily powerful tool, but its outputs are only as reliable as its inputs and assumptions. The best risk managers combine Monte Carlo with stress testing, regime detection, and healthy skepticism about their own models. As George Box said: "All models are wrong, but some are useful."'
