      1,
      'Correct! When the spread widens, you short the relatively expensive stock and long the relatively cheap one, betting the spread will revert to its historical mean of $5. Your profit is the convergence of the spread, regardless of whether both stocks go up or down.',
      'Not quite. In pairs trading, when the spread widens beyond its historical norm, you short the overpriced security and long the underpriced one. The profit comes from the spread narrowing back to its mean, making this a market-neutral bet on convergence, not market direction.'
