        { date: '1944', event: 'Kiyosi Itô publishes his stochastic integral and Itô\'s lemma', context: 'Working in wartime Japan with limited access to Western mathematics, Itô developed the stochastic integral and the chain rule for random processes. He had no idea his work would revolutionize finance, he was solving pure mathematical problems.' },
        { date: '1973', event: 'Black-Scholes-Merton use Itô\'s lemma to derive the options pricing formula', context: 'Fischer Black, Myron Scholes, and Robert Merton applied Itô\'s lemma to a portfolio of an option and its underlying stock, showing that the random terms cancel through hedging. The result: a deterministic PDE whose solution gives the exact fair price of any European option. The CBOE opens the same year.' },
        { date: '1973, Present', event: 'Stochastic calculus becomes the universal language of derivatives pricing', context: 'Every subsequent model, Heston stochastic volatility, Hull-White interest rates, jump-diffusion models, is built on Itô\'s framework. The global derivatives market grew from zero to $600+ trillion notional value. Stochastic calculus is now required knowledge for every quant on Wall Street.' }
