            { label: 'Reject scaling. At $3B, the square-root law predicts costs of ~6.9% (4% × √3), exceeding the 6% alpha. The strategy has a capacity limit near $1.5-2B', consequence: 'Correct. The square-root law means impact grows with √(AUM). At 3× AUM, costs increase by √3 ≈ 1.73×, from 4% to ~6.9%, exceeding the 6% alpha. The strategy\'s capacity is the AUM where impact equals alpha: $1B × (6%/4%)² ≈ $2.25B maximum. Beyond this, net alpha is negative. Capacity awareness is critical for strategy viability.', score: 3 },
            { label: 'Scale to $3B but trade less frequently to offset costs', consequence: 'Trading less frequently reduces costs but also reduces alpha capture (signals decay without trading). You can\'t simply halve turnover and keep the same alpha, the relationship is non-linear. The fundamental capacity constraint remains.', score: 1 }
