      { term: 'Numeraire', definition: 'The asset used as the unit of account for pricing. Changing the numeraire changes the risk-neutral measure. The standard numeraire is the money market account (growing at rate r), but using bonds or other assets as numeraire simplifies pricing of specific instruments.', example: 'Using the zero-coupon bond as numeraire leads to the T-forward measure, which simplifies pricing of forward-starting options and interest rate derivatives.', sentence: 'The choice of numeraire is a powerful technique: it can transform a complex multi-factor pricing problem into a simpler single-factor problem by absorbing common factors into the measure.' },
      { term: 'Equivalent Martingale Measure', definition: 'A probability measure Q equivalent to P (same events have zero/nonzero probability) under which discounted asset prices are martingales. Existence of Q is equivalent to no-arbitrage.', example: 'Under Q, the discounted stock price e^(-rt)S_t is a martingale: E^Q[e^(-rT)S_T] = e^(-rt)S_t. This means the discounted stock has zero expected drift under Q, no predictable gains.', sentence: 'The equivalent martingale measure is the mathematical bridge between no-arbitrage theory and pricing practice, its existence guarantees that every derivative has a well-defined fair price.' },
      { term: 'Stochastic Discount Factor', definition: 'The Radon-Nikodym derivative dQ/dP that converts real-world probabilities to pricing probabilities. It encodes investor risk preferences and assigns high weight to bad states.', example: 'In a crash state, dQ/dP might be 2.0 (Q double-weights crashes). In a boom state, dQ/dP might be 0.5 (Q half-weights booms). This makes crash protection (puts) expensive under Q.', sentence: 'The stochastic discount factor connects asset pricing theory to economics, it shows that risk-neutral pricing implicitly accounts for risk aversion by reweighting probabilities toward bad outcomes.' },
      { term: 'Market Completeness', definition: 'A market is complete if every contingent claim can be replicated by dynamic trading in existing assets. Completeness implies the risk-neutral measure Q is unique.', example: 'Black-Scholes is complete: trade stock + bond to replicate any option. Stochastic vol models are incomplete: you need the stock, bond, AND a vol instrument to replicate all claims.', sentence: 'Market completeness determines whether derivatives have unique prices or a range of arbitrage-free prices, in incomplete markets, the "correct" price depends on which equivalent measure you choose.' },
      { term: 'Volatility Surface', definition: 'The three-dimensional surface of implied volatilities across strikes and maturities. It encodes the market\'s Q-measure distribution and deviations from Black-Scholes lognormality.', example: 'The vol surface might show: ATM 1-month = 20%, ATM 1-year = 22%, 80% strike 1-month = 28%, 80% strike 1-year = 26%. The skew and term structure reveal crash risk and mean reversion.', sentence: 'The volatility surface is the market\'s most complete message about risk, it reveals the Q-measure distribution at every horizon and every loss scenario, far richer than any single number.' }
