      {label:'No, under risk-neutral pricing, use the risk-free rate r as the drift, regardless of the stock\'s actual expected return. The option price is independent of μ because it can be hedged',outcome:'Correct. The entire point of risk-neutral pricing is that the derivative\'s hedged value doesn\'t depend on μ. Use dS = rS dt + σS dW^Q for pricing. The stock\'s real-world expected return is irrelevant for derivative pricing. Only r and σ matter.',correct:true},
      {label:'Use the average of μ and r as a compromise',outcome:'There is no compromise needed, the mathematics is precise. The hedging argument proves that the price depends on r and σ only. Using anything other than r for the drift produces an arbitrage opportunity. The risk-neutral drift IS the risk-free rate, period.',correct:false}
