            { label: 'Explain that hedging eliminates dependence on μ: since you can replicate the option by delta-hedging with the stock, the hedge portfolio is riskless and must earn r. The option price follows from no-arbitrage, not from expected returns', consequence: 'Correct. The key insight: the option CAN be hedged, making it redundant (replicable from traded assets). A redundant asset\'s price is determined by replication cost, not expected return. Whether the stock returns 15% or 2%, the hedge portfolio produces the same P&L. This is why only r and σ matter for option pricing.', score: 3 },
            { label: 'Use the average of 5% and 15% as a practical compromise', consequence: 'There is no valid compromise. Risk-neutral pricing is exact, the hedge portfolio argument proves the price depends on r and σ only. Any drift other than r is wrong and creates arbitrage. This is not a matter of opinion; it\'s a mathematical theorem.', score: 0 }
