            { label: 'Black-Scholes assumes constant volatility, but the market\'s Q-measure (reflected in the vol smile) includes fat tails, skewness, and stochastic vol. Barrier options are highly sensitive to the vol surface, so your constant-vol model underprices the true risk', consequence: 'Correct. Barrier options depend on the entire distribution of paths, making them extremely sensitive to deviations from lognormality. The vol smile tells you the market believes in fatter tails and more extreme moves than constant-vol Black-Scholes assumes. Using a stochastic vol or local vol model calibrated to the vol surface would give a price closer to $4.20.', score: 3 },
            { label: 'Add 70 cents as a "model adjustment factor"', consequence: 'Ad hoc adjustments are dangerous because they don\'t capture the risk correctly. The right approach: use a model that matches the vol surface (local vol, stochastic vol, or jump-diffusion) so the Q-measure distribution is realistic. Then the model price will naturally match the market.', score: 1 }
