            { label: 'Less than $100M, risk-neutral pricing assumes continuous hedging. In an illiquid market, you cannot hedge, so the position carries risk that the model doesn\'t price. The "fair value" should include a liquidity/model risk discount reflecting the inability to replicate', consequence: 'Correct. Risk-neutral pricing = pricing by replication. No replication (illiquid markets) = no reliable risk-neutral price. The $100M is the theoretical value IF you could hedge. The actual value must discount for: (1) hedging errors from discontinuous trading, (2) wider bid-ask spreads, (3) the possibility of forced liquidation at distressed prices. This is exactly what destroyed LTCM.', score: 3 },
            { label: 'More than $100M, distressed markets create opportunities', consequence: 'While distressed markets may create long-term opportunities, the immediate portfolio value is lower, not higher, than model price. You hold unhedged risk in an illiquid market, the value should reflect that additional risk, not ignore it.', score: 0 }
