      'The Gaussian Copula and the $2 Trillion Disaster',
      [
        { date: '2000', event: 'David Li publishes "On Default Correlation: A Copula Function Approach" at JPMorgan', context: 'Li\'s paper provided an elegant, tractable model for correlated defaults. Instead of modeling the complex dynamics of each borrower, map default times to Gaussian variables and correlate them. The Gaussian copula reduced portfolio credit risk to a single parameter: correlation ρ. Wall Street finally had a standard pricing tool for CDOs.' },
        { date: '2001-2006', event: 'The structured credit market explodes from $275B to $4.7T, powered by Gaussian copula pricing', context: 'The model made CDO pricing "simple", plug in a correlation, compute tranche prices. Rating agencies used it to assign AAA ratings to senior tranches. Banks manufactured CDOs at industrial scale. The model\'s reassurance that senior tranches were safe enabled massive leverage. CDOs-of-CDOs (CDO²) were created, amplifying the dependency assumption error.' },
        { date: '2007-2008', event: 'Housing defaults begin clustering; Gaussian copula predictions fail catastrophically', context: 'As housing prices fell nationally (an event the model assigned near-zero probability), defaults became massively correlated. Borrowers in different states, different income levels, different mortgage types all defaulted simultaneously, because they shared a common factor (housing prices) that the single-correlation Gaussian copula inadequately captured. Senior tranches that were "supposed" to be safe lost 50-100%.' },
        { date: 'Aftermath', event: 'Over $2T in losses; Li later says "The most dangerous part is when people believe everything coming out of the model"', context: 'The crisis led to fundamental reassessment of copula models in finance. The industry moved toward: multi-factor models, t-copulas with tail dependency, scenario analysis that doesn\'t rely on a single copula, and stress testing that assumes dependency structures can change in crises. Li himself was not the villain, his paper explicitly noted limitations. The failure was institutional: using a model beyond its valid range because it gave convenient answers.' }
      ],
      'The Gaussian copula saga contains the deepest lesson in quantitative finance: models are tools, not truths. The copula was mathematically elegant and practically useful, within its domain of validity. The catastrophe occurred when the model was applied beyond its limits, to situations where its key assumption (no tail dependency) was dangerously wrong. Every quant must understand not just how their models work, but when they fail. The cost of misunderstanding model limitations: $2 trillion and a global financial crisis.'
