      {label:'Reject the Gaussian copula for senior tranche pricing. Require a t-copula or Clayton copula analysis with calibrated tail dependency to see the true senior tranche risk, which is likely 50-100× higher than the Gaussian estimate',outcome:'Correct. Senior tranche risk is dominated by tail dependency, exactly what Gaussian misses. With a t-copula (ν=5, ρ=0.3), senior tranche loss probability might be 1-5% instead of 0.02%. With Clayton copula (θ=1.5), it might be 3-10%. The 50-1000× difference determines whether the tranche is truly AAA or fundamentally mispriced. This analysis would have prevented the worst CDO losses.',correct:true},
      {label:'Use the Gaussian copula but increase ρ to 0.8 to be conservative',outcome:'Increasing ρ within the Gaussian copula increases dependency on average but STILL produces zero tail dependency. The Gaussian copula at ρ = 0.8 understates tail risk compared to the t-copula at ρ = 0.3 with ν = 4. The problem is the copula FAMILY (Gaussian), not the correlation parameter.',correct:false}
