            { label: 'The 0.001% estimate is almost certainly far too low. Replace with a t-copula (ν=5-8) and compare: the joint crash probability will likely be 0.1-1%, which matches the empirical frequency of such events (several times per century)', consequence: 'Correct. Empirically, simultaneous 20%+ declines in diversified equity portfolios occur every 5-15 years, implying a probability of 1-3% per year, not 0.001%. The Gaussian copula understates this by 1000×. A t-copula with ν = 5-8 produces estimates of 0.1-1%, much closer to empirical reality. Risk limits, capital reserves, and stress tests should be based on the realistic estimate.', score: 3 },
            { label: 'Add a 10× safety factor to the Gaussian estimate', consequence: 'A 10× safety factor still gives 0.01%, implying once in 10,000 years. The real frequency is 1-3% per year. Ad hoc multipliers don\'t fix structural model deficiencies. Use the right copula instead.', score: 0 }
