          truth: 'While many quants have PhDs, the field is increasingly accessible to strong undergraduates with quantitative training (math, statistics, CS, physics, engineering). Skills matter more than credentials: programming (Python, C++), statistics, probability, and the ability to translate financial problems into mathematical frameworks. Many successful quants have master\'s degrees or even bachelor\'s degrees with strong quantitative skills.',
          explanation: 'Renaissance Technologies famously hired mathematicians, physicists, and computer scientists, not finance MBAs. But what they valued was problem-solving ability and mathematical intuition, not the specific degree. Today\'s quant firms hire based on coding ability (live coding interviews), mathematical reasoning (probability puzzles), and domain knowledge (financial markets). The barrier is skill, not credentials.'
